a	2026-06-28-12-POSIX-Is-Not-A-Shell2026-06-28-12-POSIX-Nao-E-Um-Shell.pt
t	POSIX Is Not A Shell
i	Alexandre Gomes Gaigalas – June 28, 2026
p	xWhen someone says "write it in POSIX shell for portability," they mean well.
p	xPOSIX is a specification. Not a program. The thing that actually runs your script is bash, dash, ash, ksh, yash, or one of a dozen others. They each implement POSIX with their own gaps, extensions, and historical accidents.
p	xHere is a small experiment. One line, no flags, no functions, nothing exotic:
c	C#!/bin/sh
c	Kecho. S"C:\new"
p	xOn bash, ksh, and a handful of others, you get back exactly what you typed:
c	.C:\new
p	xOn dash (which is m/bin/shx on Debian, Ubuntu, and Alpine) the same line prints:
c	.C:
c	.ew
p	xdash's mechox interprets m\nx as a newline. bash's does not. Run it across kshell-versionsd (https://github.com/alganet/shell-versions)x and the shells split almost evenly: about half treat the backslash literally, the other half expand it.
c	Kdocker. run --rm alganet/shell-versions:all -c S'echo "C:\new"'
p	xPOSIX does not break the tie. The standard explicitly leaves kecho's treatment of backslash escapes implementation-definedd (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/echo.html#tag_20_37_05)x, and kencourages you to use printf insteadd (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/echo.html#tag_20_37_16)x. So "POSIX mechox" is not a behavior you can write a script against. It is a documented disagreement.
p	xThis is not a contrived edge case. mechox is the first command anyone learns. It is what "POSIX compliant" means in practice: compliant with the parts the spec actually pins down, on the versions that happened to ship.
h	The Problem With Dialects
p	xNatural languages have dialects. Brazilian Portuguese and European Portuguese are both Portuguese. A native speaker of one can understand the other with effort, but they are not the same. You cannot write Portuguese and expect it to work identically everywhere.
p	xShell scripting has the same problem. Bash is not the shell. It is a shell with a specific set of behaviors, many of which are not in POSIX and some of which contradict implementations that are technically more compliant (like dash).
p	xThe community pretends otherwise. We say "sh script" and mean "bash with m-e -u -o pipefailx." We say "POSIX compliant" and mean "it worked in CI." We say "portable" and mean "it ran on the two machines I tried."
h	What Validation Actually Looks Like
p	xI have been building kshell-docsd (https://github.com/alganet/shell-docs)x, a cross-shell reference that validates each documented feature across 14 shells. The process is mechanical: write an example, run it on every shell, record the output, check for divergence.
p	xMost features work the same. Some do not.
p	m$#x - the count of positional parameters. Works everywhere, no surprises.
p	mlocalx - not in POSIX at all. Every shell has it anyway, with different scoping rules.
p	m$(( ))x - karithmetic expansiond (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_04)x, universally supported. Division by zero: varies by implementation.
p	m[[ ]]x - not POSIX, not present in dash. If your script uses it under m#!/bin/shx, it will silently fail on every system where m/bin/shx is dash (Debian, Ubuntu, Alpine).
p	xThe ones that diverge are not bugs. They are accumulated decisions from decades of independent implementations, each targeting a slightly different point in the spec's history: 1988, 2001, 2017.
h	The Honest Version of "Portable"
p	xPortable means: tested across the range of shells it will actually run on.
p	xThe tool kshell-versionsd (https://github.com/alganet/shell-versions)x tracks what version of each shell ships with major distributions. At time of writing, m/bin/shx is:
b	mdash 0.5.12x on Ubuntu 24.04
b	mbash 3.2.57x on macOS (unchanged in fifteen years; GPL v3 kept bash 4.x out of the base system)
b	mbusybox ashx on Alpine
b	mksh93x on some enterprise Linux distributions
p	xThese are not the same program. They are not even the same age.
p	xIf you write m#!/bin/bashx and mean it, that is fine. At least it is honest. Write m#!/bin/shx and you are promising something you should verify.
h	Verification Is A Habit
p	xThe validation harness in shell-docs runs each example against all 14 shells in isolation, captures stdout and exit codes, and compares them against a known-good table. When a new shell version is released, you run it again.
p	xThis is not complex. It is just work that does not happen by default.
p	xThe payoff is that "it works in POSIX sh" can mean something concrete: it works in the fourteen shells I tested, and here are the results. That is a different claim than "I ran it in bash and it looked fine."
p	xPOSIX is not a shell. It is a promise about what shells should do. Verification is how you find out which ones kept it.
r	
